The C provider creates CMake applications and static libraries. For a guided first build, follow [Your first C project](/docs/first-c-project/).

## Generated project

| Choice or file | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| Language mode | C17 by default; C11 and C23 also available |
| Library | `<name>_core`, built from `src/core.c` |
| Public header | `include/<name>.h`, with `extern "C"` guards for C++ consumers |
| Application | Optional `<name>` executable from `app/main.c`; prints `42` |
| Test | `<name>_test` checks addition and cancellation to zero through CTest |
| Build registration | `cmake/RivetTargets.cmake`, regenerated by file and target operations |

CMake sets `C_STANDARD_REQUIRED YES` and disables compiler extensions. The compiler must support the selected mode. Presets, quickfix diagnostics, and executable discovery use the shared [CMake task workflow](/docs/cmake/).

## File and target operations

`:RivetFile` offers a C source, header, or pair. Bare source names go under `src/`; bare headers go under `include/`. A path containing `/` is relative to the project root. Files join the selected target, and pairs include the generated header from the source.

`:RivetTarget` adds an executable or static library. Custom dependencies belong in `CMakeLists.txt` or an included CMake file.

## Mixed C and C++

In a Rivet C++ project, `c_source`, `c_header`, and `c_pair` are available through the C++ file provider. Selecting the C provider also offers source, header, and pair choices for a C++ target.

```sh
rivet file interop --kind c_pair --target simulation_core
```

This example requires an existing `simulation_core` target. A C source enables C and requests C17 independently of the C++ standard. C headers provide `extern "C"` guards; declarations and definitions must use matching names. C sources receive no C++ module imports.
